.TH CLOG_DERIVE 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
clog_derive \- create a derived logger sharing the same output target
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/clogger.h>

clog clog_derive(clog parent);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Creates a new derived logger that writes to the same file descriptor as
parent and shares the same log-rotation state and write-serialising lock.
All writes from parent and every derived logger are therefore serialised
by that same lock and land in the same destination.

At the moment of the call, the derived logger gets a snapshot of the
structured fields and the minimum log level of parent.  After that, the
two loggers keep fully independent field maps and level settings.  To
add, to remove or to change a field or the level on one logger has no
effect on the other.

The root logger keeps control of the log rotation, when the original
logger on a file has one configured.  A derived logger gets the benefit
of it transparently.

Release a derived logger with clog_close() when you no longer need it.
The file below stays open until every handle is closed.  That covers
the root and every derived logger.  The shared backing store uses a
refcount.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
parent  Source logger to derive from.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
Returns a new derived logger handle on success.
Returns CLOG_INVALID if memory allocation fails.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
A derived logger inherits the output format and the syslog facility
from the shared backing store.  When parent logs asynchronously, the
derived logger also logs asynchronously, through the same writer
thread.  The derived logger allocates through the allocator of the
root logger.  A change to the output format or the syslog facility,
through any handle, is immediately visible to all handles that write
to the same fd.
Thread-safe.  The library synchronises the snapshot of the fields
against a concurrent clog_set_field(3), clog_remove_field(3) or
clog_clear_fields(3) call on parent.  It does so whatever write is in
progress on a sibling logger.
Asserts when parent is CLOG_INVALID, stale or already closed.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR clog_open_fd_mp (3),
.BR clog_open_file_mp (3),
.BR clog_close (3)
